Optionally drop the CJK encodings from mbstring to shrink the image.

The bundled libmbfl carries the legacy CJK (Chinese/Japanese/Korean) codecs and their
tables in mbfilter_cjk.c. With the mbstring-cjk option off, the sdk leaves that file (and
nls_ja/kr/zh.c) out of the build and defines MBSTRING_NO_CJK; this patch then drops, behind
the macro, everything that would otherwise reference the missing encodings: the CJK entries
in the encoding registry, the ja/ko/zh-cn/zh-tw languages (whose detect lists and mail
charsets are CJK encodings, so mb_language() rejects them with a ValueError), the sjis_mac
fast-path check in mb_substr, and the mb_convert_kana() function (Japanese kana, backed by
a table in mbfilter_cjk.c). The name lookup uses a perfect hash that indexes the full
registry, so without the CJK entries it searches the names instead and the hash tables are
left out. UTF-8/UTF-16/Latin and everything else keep working. Without the macro this is a
no-op (all encodings, languages and mb_convert_kana present).
